A||||2||CUTTER||How to get a home paint job looking shiney?||||1||
Z||000000||CUTTER||05-07-2000||10:41 PM||||I painted my front nose, spoiler, and fender with spray cans and a clear coat, but it has sorta a rough eggshell type feel too it. It is not as shiney as original and wanted to know how to get it looking shiney? Should I use like 2000 grit wet sand-paper and sand it lightly? I heard to do that and then buff it. Should I? any ideas?<BR>Thanks, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fret Naught||05-08-2000||12:33 AM||||Well Cutter, that is the Idea, the problem with poof cans is it is hard to get a good thick even coat on such a big area, so when you wet sand and buff you have to be real careful not to sand or burn through the finish. Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||05-08-2000||12:54 AM||||I'd go over the whole thing with 400,wipe clean with alcohol,then spray as even of a coat of clear as possible,using a fresh can down to about the halfway point,then grab another can.You can use the rest of the cans for anything but the last coat.Two good wet coats will usually flow out pretty nice without runs,if you're careful.A good thickness can be achieved by three applications,a day apart,lightly sanded between coats,then...<BR>Let it cure for about a week,then wet sand,starting with 800,then 1000,then 1200,or 1500.After that a  super fine polish,and some wax.<BR>Don't even go near the edges with any sanding or polishing; they just get done by doing the flat surfaces.<BR>You would have a tough time doing a hood like this,but the panels you mentioned are all small enough to work out using a can.||||reg||1
